# Sequelize `include` by association name

Authority (example): `docs/platforms/backend/contracts/meeting-live-state.md` §1.3 (`buildLiveParticipants`), `docs/platforms/backend/contracts/meeting-domain.md` §9.1 (Ability notify-template roster load).

## Rule

When eager-loading a related model, identify the association by its **registered name** (`as` / default plural), not by passing a model factory/class.

Allowed:

```ts
include: ["member"]
include: [{ association: "member", required: true }]
include: [{ association: "participants", include: ["member"] }]
```

Forbidden:

```ts
include: [Member()]
include: [Member]
include: [{ model: Member() }]
```

## Why

Association names match how the relation was booted (`belongsTo` / `hasMany` `as`). Model-class includes are easy to mis-wire (wrong association, missing `as`, silent empty nests) and diverge from mixin helpers like `meeting.getParticipants(...)`.

## When touching includes

1. Prefer the association name already used on the parent (`member`, `participants`, `organization`, …).
2. Use `{ association, required?, include? }` when you need options.
3. Keep GQL bridge `ident` / ORM `as` aligned when the include name is also a GraphQL nest field.
